  "interpretation": [
    "The source-level comparison charges equal complete caller outputs. No hidden width decoding/admission was found.",
    "c9g direct16 binary search uses conditional selects for endpoint decisions. Widths8 has the same checkpoint loop then a scalar cumulative width walk with data-dependent exit.",
    "The selected 16 widths native reverse candidate is justified by actual local scan work and the roughly 1.86 to 1.91 times reverse cost at 4M/16M sparse N256. It is still unimplemented and unmeasured here.",
    "Queries are blocked: 256 probes reuse one N256 geometry block. Direct geometry is 514 B/block; widths plus checkpoints 324 B/block. This amortizes population-level misses and prevents extrapolation to unrelated-row point probes.",
    "Shuffled projection native prefix is correctly guarded by both live and nonzero used length. It still reconstructs start and loses vector/pointer registers across real memcpy calls. The approximately 9 to 10 percent large-set width penalty is complete composed projection cost.",
    "Physical projection only streams widths with a running cursor; it pays no checkpoint reconstruction or SIMD prefix. Large physical differences are small and variable.",
    "Allocated body remains 64 B/input rank and common sidecars 2.1328125 B/input rank at N256. Width geometry 1.265625 B/rank vs direct 2.0078125 B/rank saves 37 percent of geometry but only about 1.09 percent of all allocated planes; reverse does not touch body/used.",
    "No mutable PMU capture was taken. Instruction dependencies and branch placement are observed; branch misses, LLC/DRAM service, and projected native speedups are not measured."
  ],
